    "tranche": "T4",
    "tranche_reason": "The paper compares loss and downstream results across three model configurations at similar theoretical training compute, but does not fit or reproduce a scaling law.",
    "gate_note": "The paper reports held-out ARXIV token NLL and downstream benchmark scores for three final checkpoints, plus training-loss endpoints. It does not sweep parameters, tokens, or compute over several orders of magnitude, fit exponents, test extrapolation, or search for a compute-optimal parameter/token split. Its training-loss results are not held-out loss. It states that the results do not establish a scaling law.",
    "test_reason": "The study uses scaling as motivation and compares its proposed SST method with two classic baselines at approximately equal theoretical training compute. It reports method performance, without testing a power-law form, extrapolation, optimal allocation, or a predictive relationship between scale or pretraining loss and downstream performance. The authors explicitly disclaim establishing a scaling law.",
    "authors_conclusion": "The record's paraphrase is accurate: SST achieves lower training loss at comparable theoretical cumulative training compute and higher scores on the seven reported downstream tasks. Its analytical inference-cost advantage depends on the prefill/decode mix. The authors explicitly say these observations establish neither a scaling law, causal benefits of individual ingredients, nor measured serving speedup.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "The paper compares final checkpoints from a staged KITE/SST expansion route with classic-trained baselines; it does not fit power laws or compute-optimal allocation exponents as in the original scaling-law studies.",
